    {
      "id": "rn_yk3Y4mnTjrQkDgpd::L2::Q8",
      "moduleId": "rn_yk3Y4mnTjrQkDgpd::L2",
      "stem": "Explain why gas formation is the strongest entropy signal in an equation.",
      "misconceptionClass": "gas signal why",
      "kind": "explain-why",
      "modelAnswer": "Gas particles occupy enormously more positional arrangements than condensed-phase particles, so any change that creates a gas from solids or liquids multiplies the arrangement count by the largest factor ordinary chemistry offers.",
      "keywords": [
        "gas",
        "arrangements",
        "positional"
      ],
      "options": []
    },
    {
      "id": "rn_yk3Y4mnTjrQkDgpd::L2::Q9",
      "moduleId": "rn_yk3Y4mnTjrQkDgpd::L2",
      "stem": "An ionic solid dissolves exothermically. What can be said about the balance of lattice breaking and hydration ordering in this case?",
      "misconceptionClass": "application",
      "kind": "application",
      "modelAnswer": "The hydration ordering and energy release outweigh the lattice-breaking term strongly, showing that the solvent-ordering effect can dominate; the entropy change could still be either sign, so both contributions must be weighed.",
      "keywords": [
        "hydration",
        "lattice",
        "ordering"
      ],
      "options": []
    },
    {
